LOGIN(1)                    User Commands

NAME
    login - start a session on the terminal

SYNOPSIS
    login [name]

DESCRIPTION
    login runs as root on the console, started by the greeter(1) on a
    machine without a display or by init when the console entry of
    init.conf(5) names it. login asks for
    an account name and its password, which is typed without echo. An
    unknown name is asked for a password like any other, which hides
    whether an account exists. After a wrong answer login waits two
    seconds, prints "Login incorrect" and asks again.

    An account without a password, such as root and user on a new system,
    logs in with an empty password and then has to choose a new one,
    typed twice, before its session starts.

    For a valid account login gives the terminal to it with mode 0620 and
    the display /dev/fb0 with mode 0600, which lets startgui run the
    display server as the account. It then takes on the account's
    supplementary groups, group and uid, enters its home directory and
    runs its shell as a login shell, with an argument zero starting with
    "-", in an environment of HOME, USER, LOGNAME, SHELL, TERM and PATH
    alone. When the shell ends, init starts login again, which returns
    the terminal and the display to root first.

FILES
    /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow

SEE ALSO
    greeter(1), su(1), sudo(1), passwd(1), init.conf(5), passwd(5), index(7)
